<TopicPage slug="subjects/landscape-architecture/foundational/landscape-design-process">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Landscape architecture is design with a living medium. Unlike a building, a landscape grows,
    moves, dies back and matures on decadal timescales; it changes by season and by year; and it
    is never "complete" in the way a completed building is. Those facts bend the whole
    discipline's process. It begins not with sketching but with <em>reading</em> — the site's
    ecology, hydrology, soils, microclimate and cultural history — because in landscape the site
    is not a blank canvas but a co-author. It proceeds through diagrams before drawings, concepts
    before details, and it ends years after construction, when the planted idea finally stands
    up and the maintenance regime the designer planned (or failed to plan) reveals itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional process, taught in every accredited programme and codified in the
    accreditation standards, runs: research the site, analyse and map it, generate a concept,
    develop the design, and deliver documents — then steward the result. Around that sequence
    sits a body of theory — a history of how the best designers have arranged land, water,
    plants and people — that every studio education transmits. This page covers the three
    foundations: how to see a site, how designs are generated in the studio, and where the
    discipline's design ideas come from.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the design process, site-first" widget="equationStep" config={processCfg} />

  <h2>Site analysis and inventory: the site is the brief</h2>
  <p>
    Two designers can walk the same lot and see different projects, but neither should see
    nothing, and the difference between seeing and merely looking is method. The profession
    splits the first phase into <strong>inventory</strong> (collecting facts) and
    <strong>analysis</strong> (interpreting them into design consequences). The inventory is
    systematic: <em>climate and sun</em> — the sun's path across the seasons (a solar geometry
    problem with real stakes: summer shade is wanted where winter light is precious),
    prevailing winds, frost pockets; <em>water</em> — where rain arrives, flows, pools and
    exits, at what rates; <em>soil</em> — profiles, drainage, compaction, bearing capacity,
    pH; <em>vegetation</em> — existing trees (which are assets to be kept, with their root
    zones protected); <em>views</em> — what deserves framing, what deserves screening;
    <em>circulation</em> — how people (and water, and wildlife) actually move, which is often
    not how the plan assumes; <em>regulation and utilities</em> — zoning, easements, buried
    lines; and <em>culture</em> — the site's history and the neighbourhood's habits. None of
    this is decoration of a report; every item is a constraint or an opportunity with a
    construction consequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The analysis phase then overlays the findings — historically on translucent sheets, today
    in GIS layers — and the discipline's signature method appears: <strong>suitability
    analysis</strong>, Ian McHarg's layered-map technique from <em>Design with Nature</em>
    (1969), in which each factor (slope, soil, drainage, habitat) is mapped and the overlays
    reveal where building, planting and play belong. What distinguishes professional analysis
    from decoration is that it generates the design rather than merely preceding it. A chronic
    drainage problem becomes a <strong>rain garden</strong> — the nuisance re-cast as a
    feature that manages stormwater on site. A blazing west-facing wall becomes a
    <strong>deciduous shade strategy</strong> — a tree that screens summer sun and drops its
    canopy to admit winter light. A noisy road edge becomes a berm-and-planting sound design.
    Good analysis, the studio maxim says, usually contains the concept; the designer's first
    job is to listen until it speaks.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that landscape design is plant selection — picking pretty species for pretty beds. Planting is one deliverable among many (grading, drainage, paving, structures, lighting), and it is decided <em>last</em>, inside a spatial and functional structure. The reverse order — shopping for plants, then finding somewhere to put them — is the amateur signature, and it fails in the first summer drought.</p>

  <h2>Design generation and the studio: from diagram to construction</h2>
  <p>
    Landscape design is learned, almost universally, in the <strong>studio</strong> — the
    drafting-room classroom where students work through full projects under repeated critique.
    The studio's method is a process and a pedagogy at once. Programming states what the design
    must achieve (uses, areas, adjacencies, budget). Concept generation begins with
    <strong>functional diagrams</strong> — bubble diagrams in which spaces are circles and
    relationships are arrows (kitchen near terrace, play visible from kitchen, service hidden) —
    because it is far cheaper to move a bubble than a wall. From the bubbles condenses a
    <strong>parti</strong>: the organising idea of the whole composition, one diagram that
    explains the design — "a spine path threading three garden rooms", "the slope terraced
    into sequential outdoor rooms". A strong parti survives the budget cuts and value
    engineering that will certainly come; a weak one dissolves into decoration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Design development</strong> then gives the concept its body, and here the living
    medium bites. <em>Grading</em> — shaping the ground itself — is the field's most
    under-appreciated art: contours are manipulated to drain water, level a terrace, screen a
    road, and create the sense of enclosure, with cut-and-fill balanced because trucking soil
    is money. <em>Planting design</em> composes in layers — canopy, understory, shrub, perennial,
    ground — each chosen for function (shade, screening, seasonal interest, habitat, stormwater
    uptake) as well as appearance, and each with mature dimensions. <em>Materials</em> — paving,
    walls, edges, furniture — must age gracefully outdoors, which is a materials-science
    constraint most beginners underestimate. And the fourth dimension, <strong>time</strong>,
    is designed explicitly: a newly planted landscape is expected to look provisional, a
    meadow's second summer to differ from its first, and a tree's real contribution to arrive
    fifteen years after the ribbon-cutting — so designers plant in succession, with
    fast-growing "filler" material planned for later removal, and they write maintenance into
    the design rather than leaving it as an afterthought. The studio's final phases —
    construction drawings and specifications (the legal documents that hand the idea to
    builders), cost estimating, and post-occupancy evaluation — convert the drawing into
    something a contractor can build and a client can maintain.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two skills from design development deserve their own worked passages, because they are
    the ones exams and job sites test. The first is <strong>grading arithmetic</strong>: the
    designer speaks in contour lines and spot elevations, and every path, terrace and lawn is
    a drainage device. A walkway must cross 0.45 m of fall over 15 m; a 5 per cent slope
    (the comfortable maximum for an accessible path) drops 5 m per 100 m — 0.75 m over 15 m —
    which is more than the site offers, so the designer either lengthens the path (switchbacks
    and ramps buy gradient with distance), terraces it with retaining walls, or regrades the
    adjacent bed. Conversely, a plaza that must drain <em>to</em> a rain garden is drafted
    with a minimum 1–2 per cent fall toward the inlet — too flat and water ponds; too steep
    and it feels like a ramp. The calculation is elementary; the judgement — where to spend
    fall, where to buy it back, where walls earn their cost — is landscape architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second is <strong>planting design as composition over time</strong>. The palette is
    assembled in functional and visual layers — canopy for structure and summer shade,
    understory trees for mid-scale, shrubs for enclosure, perennials and grasses for seasonal
    rhythm, groundcover for the weed-suppressing carpet — and every plant is placed by mature
    size, which obliges the designer to draw the garden that will exist in year ten, not the
    one at installation. Practically this means spacing that looks gappy on day one (hence
    the temporary "filler" plantings scheduled for removal), irrigation zones matched to
    plants' water needs rather than to the plan's geometry, soil preparation specified like
    any other subgrade, and a maintenance regime written into the documents — because a
    meadow unmowed at the right moment becomes a thicket, and a hedge unpruned becomes a
    monolith. The best planting plans are, in effect, a budget of future labour; the worst
    are a first-summer photograph.
  </p>

  <h2>History of landscape design: the canon every studio argues with</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's design vocabulary is inherited, and the canon is worth knowing because
    every contemporary project implicitly takes a position in it. The <strong>formal
    tradition</strong> — Persian paradise gardens, Italian Renaissance terraces, and its
    apotheosis in French baroque: André Le Nôtre's Versailles (from the 1660s) — imposed
    geometry on land: axes, parterres, fountains, clipped hedging, the landscape as an
    expression of order and dominion. The <strong>English landscape garden</strong> of the
    eighteenth century (William Kent, Lancelot "Capability" Brown, Humphry Repton) rejected
    the geometric in favour of the apparently natural: rolling lawns, serpentine lakes,
    clumps and belts of trees, the <em>ha-ha</em> sunken wall that kept livestock out without
    a visible fence — designed scenery that looked like untouched countryside, and became the
    dominant park aesthetic exported worldwide. The <strong>Japanese garden tradition</strong>,
    running in parallel for centuries, contributed an opposite grammar of asymmetry,
    compression, borrowed scenery (shakkei) and symbolic abstraction — rocks as mountains,
    raked gravel as water — a vocabulary that shaped modernism's minimalism. The
    <strong>nineteenth-century public park</strong> turned the private estate idiom into
    democratic infrastructure: Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux's Greensward plan won
    the Central Park competition in 1858 and argued, with lasting influence, that scenery in
    cities is a public-health instrument — the founding act of landscape architecture as a
    modern profession. The <strong>modern movement</strong> of the mid-twentieth century
    (Thomas Church's California gardens, Garrett Eckbo, Dan Kiley) stripped ornament and
    composed gardens as <em>outdoor rooms</em> for modern living. And the <strong>ecological
    turn</strong> — McHarg's <em>Design with Nature</em> (1969) foremost — redefined the
    designer's brief from scenery to system: read the natural processes, design with them,
    measure success in function as well as beauty; contemporary practice (from stormwater
    parks to landscape urbanism, argued by critics such as Elizabeth Meyer) is that turn's
    ongoing argument with itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents define current practice. <strong>Scale</strong>: the same process
    runs from a residential garden to a city's open-space framework, but at metropolitan scale
    the tools shift to GIS-based suitability modelling, green-infrastructure planning and
    policy — the discipline's quiet expansion from making places to governing them.
    <strong>Evidence and performance</strong>: contemporary landscapes are expected to deliver
    measured outcomes — litres of stormwater retained per storm, degrees of urban-heat
    reduction under canopy, biodiversity indices, documented effects on wellbeing — turning
    post-occupancy evaluation from courtesy into discipline, and planting palettes into
    hydrological and ecological instruments (native and climate-adapted species, layered
    habitat structure, reduced irrigation demand). <strong>Adaptation</strong>: climate
    change is rewriting the base data — hardiness zones shifting, storm intensities rising,
    drought regimes hardening — so the field's site-first method is being applied at the
    scale of decades: designing for the tree that will mature in a hotter city, the drainage
    that must take tomorrow's cloudburst. The studio critic's oldest question — "what is
    your organising idea, and does the site agree with it?" — survives every technology
    layer, from tracing paper to parametric modelling, because it is the discipline itself:
    read the land, propose a relationship between it and human life, and take responsibility
    for the decades the answer will take to grow.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> LAAB Accreditation Standards; Booth &amp; Hiss, <em>Residential Landscape Architecture</em>; McHarg, <em>Design with Nature</em> (1969); Meyer, "The Expanded Field of Landscape Architecture."</p>
